WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:02.750
- [Instructor] So what
we have in this chart

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:06.230
is all the numbers from
one to 100 organized

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:07.500
in a fairly neat way.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.970
It's a somewhat intuitive
way to organize it

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:12.190
where each row you have
10, so you go from one

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:16.890
to 10, then 11 to 20, then
21 to 30, all the way to 100.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:18.960
And what we're gonna
look at is interesting

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.340
patterns that might emerge from this.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:23.290
So if you look at what's highlighted here

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:26.590
in this purplish color, what
numbers are highlighted there?

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:29.080
Pause this video and think about it.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:32.400
Well, what's highlighted
are all of the even numbers.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.040
And you can see the even
numbers form these nice,

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.420
neat pillars or columns on this chart.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:40.850
And we can look at that
and immediately start

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:42.460
to see some patterns.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:45.960
For example, what numbers are always going

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.090
to be in the ones place for an even number

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:50.780
looking at this chart?

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:53.040
Well, you can see in the
ones place you're either

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.080
always going to have a
two in the ones place

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:00.630
or you're going to have
a four or you're going

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:03.590
to have a six or you're
going to have an eight

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:06.150
or you're going to have a zero.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:08.540
So that ones place digit is always going

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:10.680
to be an even number.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:12.023
Let's do another example.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:15.680
Here we've highlighted different numbers.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:17.580
So pause this video and
think about what's true

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.980
about all of the numbers
that we've highlighted?

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:24.220
Well, you might notice that
these are all multiples of five.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:28.900
Five, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30,
35, 40, so on and so forth.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:33.290
And so these form these
two columns on this chart.

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:36.410
And here we can see very
clearly that multiples

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:40.170
of five are either going to have a five

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:44.240
in the ones place like
we have right over here.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:45.760
So they're either gonna have a five

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.460
in the ones place or
they're gonna have a zero

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:50.410
in the ones place.

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:51.970
You might've realized
that before but you see

00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:55.850
it very clearly in these
two, you see it very

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:58.573
clearly in these two columns.

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:01.423
Let's do one other example.

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.990
This one is really
interesting because it's not

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:08.160
just one of those clean
column-type patterns.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:09.670
It looks like we started
one, and then we have

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:11.995
this diagonal, then we go to 100.

00:02:11.995 --> 00:02:14.778
What's a pattern that
could describe how we go

00:02:14.778 --> 00:02:17.546
from one number to the
next, or another way

00:02:17.546 --> 00:02:20.170
of saying it, what's a rule for why

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:22.350
we highlighted these numbers in purple?

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:24.200
Pause the video and think about that.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:27.070
All right, well, one thing is if we go

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:28.200
from one number to the next, you go

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.560
from one to 10, we add nine.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:34.120
To go from 10 to 19, we add nine.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:37.150
To go from 19 to 28, we added nine.

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:40.920
So each number is nine
plus the previous one.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.160
And if you go all the
way to 91, 91 plus nine

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:44.840
is of course 100.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:46.280
Now, it's important to
realize these are not

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:50.410
multiples of nine because we
started at one, not at zero.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:53.070
If you started at zero,
you go zero, nine, 18,

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:56.140
so forth and so on until
you go nine, 18, 27,

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:59.060
36, 45, 54, 63, 72, 81.

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:01.240
Those would've been the multiples of nine.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:03.210
But everything got
shifted because we started

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:04.410
at one, not at zero.

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:06.780
So we go from one and then 10, 19, 28,

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:10.570
all the way down this diagonal
and then we go back to 100.

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:12.660
And so this is a really interesting
thing to think about it.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:17.130
These are all the
multiples of nine plus one

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:18.610
is another way to think about it

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:20.980
or this is if we started
at one and we keep

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:23.350
adding nines, these are all the numbers

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:24.810
that we would highlight.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:26.610
But you can see a pattern.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:29.460
Whenever you add nine
to a number, the value

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:32.300
of the ones place decreases by one.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:34.420
You see that here as you
go down these diagonals.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:39.420
You go from nine, eight,
seven, six, five, four,

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:41.950
three, two, one.

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:44.280
And even up here, we started at zero

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:46.350
but you don't have a lower digit than zero

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:47.680
so it just goes back to nine.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:50.210
So it goes zero, then goes up to nine,

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:51.970
then it keeps going
down, down, down, down,

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:54.280
all the way until it gets to zero again

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:57.230
and then it starts going
down from there again.

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:59.570
So once again, interesting
patterns to look at.

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:01.040
I encourage you to look
at a chart like this

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:03.190
and think about what
patterns can you find?

